{"kind":"report","text":"Approved K1 at 5706ac22a6a9e34bb4f8d9fa57589e7cb9ec383e, tree 6e7b0bbedf71cf09d5069bd7f28e741c357dbb04, against actual Main dba3e76237250b6ce205d511691554de3d809baf. Read primary 52966480 first in full; inspected all 122 exact-head/path artifacts with direct builder-promise provenance, exhausted all 255 current/historical exact-path query results, and prepared the assigned binding to request b1212999 / promise 1b758ab7. No findings.\n\nArchitecture: bytes owns identical record, canonical equality, natural, nullable-string and in-place owned-JSON freezing decisions. Git owns numeric byte-to-code-unit conversion, strict BOM-retaining UTF-8 and unreadable-error construction; Scope owns its bounded SQL text expression and no-store JSON replies. Domain-specific validators, equality, text, key, integer, error and byte meanings remain distinct. Existing public aliases remain available. Checked the moved implementations and their callers, identifier-only differences, schema constructors, optional/throw/false equality, negative zero versus isLocalId, metric bounds, decoder/BOM/error reset, archive error classes, response headers and SQL expressions. Destination's unrelated local record bindings remain unchanged. No schema, grant, native admission, definition pin, default cohort, storage key or existing test assertion changes. All Jam source/assets are unchanged; Counter's declaration factory rename preserves its data.\n\nSecurity: no authority expands. The application wrapper retains nosniff; MCP keeps its different response policy. Strict decoding and refusal behavior remain intact. The new helper check is source hygiene, not a hostile-code boundary. Independently ran its two Node cases and exact-source check: 506 files / 174 runtime exports; both cases and clean check pass. A new untracked file redefining projectionFailure, exported only through application-v2, is refused with exit 1; removed only my own probe afterward.\n\nSimplification: the smal